# The review ledger is APPEND-ONLY, so the only correct resolution of a conflict in
# it is "keep both sides' rows". Git's built-in `union` merge driver looks like it
# does exactly that, and it was configured here for one merge — but it is LINE-based,
# not row-based, and that difference corrupts the file.
#
# What happened, 2026-08-02, on the first real convergence:
#
#   Two rows for the same range were written by different agents with their keys in
#   different ORDER — one put `finding:` last (the file's convention), the other put
#   it before `files:`/`digest:`. `union` keeps every line from both sides in place,
#   so the two rows interleaved instead of concatenating, and a `finding:` line landed
#   inside a NEIGHBOURING row. YAML takes the last duplicate key, so that row silently
#   began reporting a different review than the one its author wrote. No conflict was
#   raised and no gate failed: `--verify-all` stayed exit 0, because a row's digest is
#   computed from the range it names, not from its own text.
#
# A silent, undetected rewrite of an audit record is strictly worse than a merge
# conflict. A conflict stops you and demands a decision; this did neither. So the
# driver is OFF, and a ledger conflict is resolved deliberately, at ROW granularity —
# see tasks/0_backlog/2026-08-02-structure-aware-merge-for-the-review-ledger/.
#
# Note this is NOT an argument against `base:` (see "A ROW'S OWN BASE" in
# automation/publish/review_gate.py). Self-describing rows are what make concatenation
# *semantically* legal at all. The defect is purely that `union` concatenates lines
# rather than rows, so it cannot honour the row boundary the semantics depend on.
